WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:06.150
With a worldwide haul of $2.7 billion dollars,
Avatar is literally the most successful film

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:07.150
ever made.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:11.440
And it's actually pretty good, too — well,
if the fans are to be believed, anyway.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.799
Cameron's gorgeous and vibrant world was something
that people just want to hang around in.

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:17.990
And when they couldn't, they got pretty bummed
out.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:22.099
"When I woke up this morning after watching
Avatar for the first time yesterday, the world

00:00:22.099 --> 00:00:23.099
seemed… gray.

00:00:23.099 --> 00:00:27.749
It was like my whole life, everything I've
done and worked for, lost its meaning."

00:00:27.749 --> 00:00:31.130
Slow down, though: that doesn't mean Avatar
was perfect.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:35.560
Between all of those dazzling special effects
were a few dumb things in Avatar that everyone

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.240
just ignored.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:38.540
Basic biology

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:42.960
Let's not even get into the science of growing
an alien hybrid clone and taking control of

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.390
it with your emotions.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:47.960
But what happens to the humans' bodies while
they're in those pods?

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:52.890
We're told Jake spends a 16-hour stint in
that little box, but how did he, like, use

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:53.890
the bathroom?

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.690
Are they wearing high-tech diapers?

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:58.440
Or are they just following Austin Powers pod
rules?

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:09.440
We don't see anyone hooked up to anything
in the box, so are they just holding it, or...is

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.440
there a drain at the bottom or something?

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.720
And to a larger extent, why not add a feeding
tube?

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:16.770
Hamsters do it.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:17.940
We can do it.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.560
Convenient straws involve science that we've
already perfected for total idiots, so why

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.100
can't we do this in the future?

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.910
Jake loses weight and is constantly reminded
to eat when he comes out of the pod.

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:33.340
They can control alien hybrids with their
mind machines, but they can't build in stuff

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.380
for basic bodily functions.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:37.920
We expect more from you, future.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.240
Do your homework

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.770
Jake is recruited to relocate to Pandora,
but he seems pretty much clueless about...

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:46.010
everything in Pandora.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:50.180
He skipped over the extensive training required
to be part of the Avatar program and just

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:51.420
read the manual instead.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.510
"How much lab training have you had?"

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:55.640
"I dissected a frog once."

00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:00.290
Everyone knows that you don't skip the tutorial,
especially if it's your real alien body on

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:01.290
the line.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:06.810
Yes, Pandora is a beautiful new world, but
Jake doesn't know his Avatar ass from his

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:08.030
Avatar elbow.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:11.999
He messes with plants that could be poisonous
and doesn't know the first thing about the

00:02:11.999 --> 00:02:15.760
indigenous animals he's tasked with protecting
his team from.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.810
Which doesn't stop him from shoving his ponytail
into one.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.730
Which is gross.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:25.620
The film obviously needs a character to serve
as a way into the story, but the dude could

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:29.260
have dug a little deeper than Pandora's Wikipedia
page.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.230
Why use Avatars at all?

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:36.389
The entire concept of creating these alien
Avatars doesn't seem like a wise way to build

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.860
real trust with an alien civilization.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:43.010
The Na'vi are smart enough to understand that
humans are different than they are, so why

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:46.760
do they think that making these "fake" versions
of the aliens will help them connect?

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.519
Haven't they seen Star Trek?

00:02:48.519 --> 00:02:52.709
Disguising yourself as an alien rarely turns
out well, and then you have to get down with

00:02:52.709 --> 00:02:55.079
a nerdy alien, and everything's a mess...

00:02:55.079 --> 00:02:57.670
"Now, will you help me?"

00:02:57.670 --> 00:02:59.040
"If you make love to me."

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:01.600
"... what?"

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:05.799
Also, if you're really trying to blend in,
you have to go native.

00:03:05.799 --> 00:03:10.090
So, why the heck do they still wear human
clothes on their Avatar bodies?

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:15.120
If you're going to all the trouble to build
this insanely expensive alien body, you probably

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.450
shouldn't dress like a tourist.

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:18.840
Lost in Pandora

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:23.760
We're asked to believe that in the distant
future, science can create alien Avatars that

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.099
can be controlled through thought … but
it can't track them?

00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:28.099
"What?"

00:03:28.099 --> 00:03:29.620
"I got a native doing the funky chicken here.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.249
He's blocking my blade."

00:03:31.249 --> 00:03:32.480
"Well, keep going.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:33.480
He'll move.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.799
These people have to learn that we don't stop."

00:03:36.799 --> 00:03:42.169
They can barely even ID their guys visually,
and almost kill Jake with their bulldozer.

00:03:42.169 --> 00:03:46.349
Even today, as the grunting Neanderthals that
we are, we can track just about everything,

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:48.299
from our dogs to our cars.

00:03:48.299 --> 00:03:49.810
So what's up, high tech future?

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:54.730
When Jake is separated from the team, they
have to scour the woods to try and find him.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:59.019
Tracking isn't that hard, even without a global
network of satellites, and especially not

00:03:59.019 --> 00:04:00.730
a century into the future.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:07.029
We've found Waldo, we've found Carmen Sandiego,
and we can find Jake and his fancy alien body.

00:04:07.029 --> 00:04:08.449
Late to the game

00:04:08.449 --> 00:04:12.309
The Na'vi have a fighting chance during the
big face-off at the end of the film because

00:04:12.309 --> 00:04:16.810
Jake travels around Pandora and rallies the
different tribes to help fend off the sky

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:17.810
people.

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:20.400
It's too bad none of the Na'vi thought of
this sooner.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.520
The entire Na'vi culture is built around a
respect for nature, but this is the first

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:28.520
time they've considered the idea of teaming
up to try and push these sky people out once

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:29.520
and for all?

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:34.270
All it took was a non-native hero to come
in and fix all of the dumb Na'vi's problems.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:39.110
Or maybe getting the Na'vi to do their own
version of the Care Bear Stare was just a

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:40.930
really weak plot contrivance.

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:42.759
The giant knife

00:04:42.759 --> 00:04:47.010
It made for a cool, bizarre moment when Colonel
Miles Quaritch's robot pulled out his own

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:51.020
giant knife during a fight, but the whole
thing is wildly impractical.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:55.770
If you want to add a bladed weapon to your
giant robot, build it right into the arm!

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:59.240
Robots are made with all kinds of awesome
contraptions and hidden doodads.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:05.060
Instead, the robot designers created a holster
for the knife, and relied on a robo-hand to

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:06.060
hold it.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:07.990
Which then -drops it.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:10.729
Even BB-8 had a lighter tucked away in his
guts.

00:05:10.729 --> 00:05:11.729
And y'know what?

00:05:11.729 --> 00:05:15.419
As far as movie knives go, it wasn't even
all that impressive…

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:17.260
"That's not a knife.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:21.460
THAT's a knife."

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.000
Thanks for watching!

00:05:24.009 --> 00:05:26.610
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:28.789
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

